<ChapterLayout
  title="7.3 — Role of the Sky in Culture · AST 100"
  description="The year, the month, the week, and the zodiac — how the sky became the first calendar, the first clock, and the first map, and why its fingerprints are all over the names of the days."
  activeId="7.3" chapter={7} current="/chapter/7/7.3">
  <Hero
    tease="Before writing, before farming, before cities, human beings were watching the sky — because it was the only reliable thing there was. The year, the month, the week, the names of the days, the direction of north: all of them were read off the sky first. Almost every unit of time you used today was handed to you by an astronomer who died before writing was invented."
    client:load />

  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="year">The Sky as a Calendar</h2>
      <p>
        Consider the problem facing a farmer eleven thousand years ago, at the very start of the curve in
        <a href={subPath("7.1")}>§7.1</a>. Plant too early and the seed rots in cold ground. Plant too late and the crop
        fails before harvest. There is no calendar on the wall — there is no wall — and weather is a liar: a warm week
        in winter tells you nothing. You need something that repeats with perfect reliability, and there is only one
        such thing in the world. It is over your head.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Sun's path through the sky changes through the year in a way that never varies. Its height at noon rises and
        falls; the point on the horizon where it rises swings north and south and back. Mark those extremes with a pair
        of stones — as people did at Stonehenge, and in a hundred other places on every continent — and you have built a
        calendar accurate to a day. Explore why it happens in the figure below.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="The year: why there are seasons">
        <SeasonsPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        The cause is the <strong>axial tilt</strong> we met in <a href={subPath("4.1")}>§4.1</a>: Earth's axis leans
        23.4° and always points the same way in space, so as we go around the Sun, each hemisphere leans alternately
        toward it and away. What matters is not the distance to the Sun but the <em>angle</em> the sunlight strikes at —
        steep light concentrates its heat, shallow light spreads it thin. It is worth being blunt here, because this is
        the most commonly believed falsehood in all of astronomy: <strong>seasons have nothing to do with distance from
        the Sun</strong>. Earth is actually <em>closest</em> to the Sun in early January, in the depth of the northern
        winter.
      </p>

      <h2 id="month">The Month, the Week, and the Zodiac</h2>
      <p>
        The Sun gives us the year and the day. The Moon gives us the other unit everyone uses. It cycles through its
        phases — new, quarter, full, quarter — in <strong>29.5 days</strong>, and that period is the origin of the
        <strong>month</strong>: the word is simply "moon", worn down by a few thousand years of use, in English and in
        many other languages. Run the cycle in the figure below.
      </p>

      <FigureFrame label="The month, the week, and the eclipse">
        <MoonPanel client:visible />
      </FigureFrame>

      <p>
        The figure also settles a question that puzzled the ancients for centuries: if the Moon passes between us and
        the Sun every single month, why isn't there an eclipse every month? Because its orbit is <strong>tilted about
        five degrees</strong> from the plane of Earth's orbit, so at most new moons it slips above or below the Sun
        rather than across it. Only when the Moon happens to be crossing that plane <em>at the same time</em> as it lines
        up do we get an eclipse. Babylonian astronomers, keeping records for centuries, spotted that eclipses repeat on
        an 18-year cycle — and could therefore <em>predict</em> them, which is an extraordinary thing to be able to do
        with no idea whatsoever of what the Moon actually is.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now, a warning about the <strong>week</strong>, because there is a tidy story that is often told and it is
        false. The seven-day week is <em>not</em> a quarter of the lunar month rounded off; a lunar quarter is 7.4 days,
        and no ancient calendar tried to force it. The week comes instead from the Jewish sabbath cycle meeting
        Hellenistic astrology, where each day was assigned to one of the <strong>seven "planets"</strong> visible to the
        naked eye: the Sun, the Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus, and Saturn. That origin is still fossilised in the
        names, in language after language:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sunday</strong> — the Sun's day; Bangla <em>Robibar</em> (Ravi, the Sun); Italian <em>Domenica</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Monday</strong> — the Moon's day; Bangla <em>Shombar</em> (Soma, the Moon); French <em>Lundi</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Tuesday</strong> — Tiw's day, the Norse Mars; Bangla <em>Mongolbar</em> (Mangal, Mars); Italian <em>Martedì</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Wednesday</strong> — Woden's day, the Norse Mercury; Bangla <em>Budhbar</em> (Budha, Mercury).</li>
        <li><strong>Thursday</strong> — Thor's day, the Norse Jupiter; Bangla <em>Brihaspatibar</em> (Brihaspati, Jupiter).</li>
        <li><strong>Friday</strong> — Frigg's day, the Norse Venus; Bangla <em>Shukrobar</em> (Shukra, Venus).</li>
        <li><strong>Saturday</strong> — Saturn's day, plainly; Bangla <em>Shonibar</em> (Shani, Saturn).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Two cultures, thousands of kilometres apart, naming the same seven days after the same seven wandering lights.
        Every time you say "Thursday" you are, without knowing it, invoking Jupiter.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those same wandering lights gave us the <strong>zodiac</strong>. The Sun, Moon, and planets all stay within a
        narrow band of sky — because, as <a href={subPath("4.1")}>§4.1</a> explained, the solar system is a flat disk
        and we are looking at it edge-on from inside. Babylonian astronomers divided that band into twelve equal
        30° slices and named each for a constellation in it. That is the origin of the star signs.
      </p>
      <p>
        And it is worth being clear about what happened next, because this course has an obligation to it.
        <strong>Astrology is not science.</strong> It makes no testable predictions that survive testing; controlled
        experiments have found no effect. It is also, on its own terms, broken: the Earth's axis wobbles slowly — a
        26,000-year cycle called <strong>precession</strong> — so the constellations have drifted roughly a full sign
        since the scheme was invented. Most people described as "Aries" had the Sun in Pisces on the day they were born.
        The Sun's path also crosses a thirteenth constellation, Ophiuchus, which astrology simply ignores. The zodiac is
        a beautiful and historically important piece of astronomy. As a theory of personality, it is a museum piece.
      </p>
      <p>
        Which leaves the deepest use of all. Every constellation is a mnemonic — a way to remember the sky well enough
        to navigate by it. The Austronesian navigators of <a href={subPath("7.1")}>§7.1</a> crossed thousands of
        kilometres of open Pacific using nothing but stars, swells, and birds, finding islands smaller than a suburb.
        The sky was humanity's first calendar, first clock, first compass, and first library — and every one of the 88
        constellations recognised today is a story that somebody, somewhere, told a child so they would remember which
        way was home.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <p><em>The year from the tilt, the month from the Moon, the week from seven wandering lights, and a map of stars
        that could carry a canoe across an ocean. We read the sky for ten thousand years to survive. Now we read it to
        ask whether anyone is reading back.</em></p>
    </div>
  </article>

